Feature: The in-app slider is a hold-music control; the phone owns how loud Bubble speaks
  DRAFT — parked per BL-233. The Node acceptance runner cannot execute Kotlin.
  Promote to a live `.feature` only when bound to step handlers that drive the
  gradle JVM unit suite (as BL-769's do); otherwise the ticket is verified by
  its recorded manual device procedure and this file stays a draft.

  BL-765 already contracted the split: the setting governs music only and the
  reply voice follows the phone's media volume. `TalkEngine` applied the same
  slider gain to both surfaces and `ReplyAudioPlayer` defaulted to 0.55, so on
  2026-08-07 a human with the phone at full volume heard replies as a whisper.
  Scenarios below cover the pure gain-routing logic; actual loudness on a
  device is device-surface behaviour and is not asserted here.

  Background:
    Given a talk engine with a hold music player and a reply player

  # BL-850 bubble-reply-volume-01
  Scenario: the reply player starts at full app gain
    When the talk engine initialises
    Then the reply player gain is full

  # BL-850 bubble-reply-volume-02
  Scenario Outline: moving the slider scales hold music only
    When the slider is set to <percent> percent
    Then the hold music gain scales to <percent> percent
    And the reply player gain is full

    Examples:
      | percent |
      | 0       |
      | 55      |
      | 100     |

  # BL-850 bubble-reply-volume-03
  Scenario: the slider is labelled for what it actually controls
    When the settings surface is rendered
    Then the volume control is labelled as the hold music volume

  # BL-850 bubble-reply-volume-04
  Scenario Outline: every reply leg is left at full gain
    Given a reply delivered over <leg>
    When the slider is set to any percent
    Then that leg is played at full gain

    Examples:
      | leg               |
      | recorded audio    |
      | text to speech    |
